{
  "dom_id": "step-q109340",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "The probability mass function of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below.\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n\n<tbody><tr> \n<td width=\"30\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr> \n<td width=\"30\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{6}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{3}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{6}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{3}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n\n<tbody><tr> \n<td width=\"30\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr> \n<td width=\"30\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$f(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{5}{8}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{8}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{4}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n<p></p></th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nFind $\\text{E}[4Y-3X].$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables (not necessarily independent), and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[aX+bY]=a\\text{E}[X]+b\\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{E}[Y].$ If $X$ is defined on a sample space ${S}_{x},$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{x}}x\\cdot f(x) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}+3\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $Y$ is defined on a sample space ${S}_{y},$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}y\\cdot f(y) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{5}{8}+5\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}+10\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[4Y-3X] & =\\text{E}[4Y]+\\text{E}[-3X] \\\\  & =4\\text{E}[Y]-3\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{15}{4}-3\\cdot \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
